==English==
{{wikipedia|sacrilege}}

===Etymology===
Compare {{m|en|sacrilege}}, {{cog|la|sacrilegus}}. From {{der|en|la|sacer}} + {{m|la|legō||steal something sacred}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/sæk.ɹəˈlɪd͡ʒ.əs/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-sacrilegious.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/sæk.ɹəˈlɪd͡ʒ.əs/|/sæk.ɹəˈlid͡ʒəs/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪd͡ʒəs|iːd͡ʒəs|s=4}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Committing [[sacrilege]]; acting or speaking very disrespectfully toward what is held to be [[sacred]].
#* {{RQ:Landon Romance|page=26|volume=III|passage=At one end of the room was spread a square carpet, and on it stood a table, on which were placed two most '''sacrilegious'''-looking wax-tapers: it is to be feared some poor sinner stayed longer in purgatory from the abduction of his offering.}}

